# Attack-path analysis
Final: low | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Evidence shows a pre-authorization metadata read (authz.rs 4256-4292) that performs SQLite queries and row materialization (node.rs 1025-1040; pg_store.rs 5335-5395) before denying access. This creates a limited availability/timing side effect without data disclosure or authorization bypass. Impact is low and exploitation requires guessing/leaking upload IDs, so the original low severity is appropriate.
## Likelihood
high - Requires network reachability and a valid/guessable upload_id; without enumeration or leakage of upload IDs, triggering the heavier path is not trivial. | Remote network vector
## Impact
low - Unauthorized callers can trigger metadata queries and row materialization before being denied, causing modest CPU/IO use and potential timing signals about upload size; no data exposure or authz bypass is shown.
## Assumptions
- argmin-s3 is deployed with ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR bound to an address reachable by untrusted clients (not just localhost).
- An attacker can send ListParts requests that reach authorize_list_parts (authenticated as a low-privileged principal or as an anonymous requester for public buckets).
- Upload IDs can be guessed or learned for at least one target upload, enabling the pre-auth list path to execute.
- Network reachability to the HTTP listener
- A ListParts request routed to authorize_list_parts
- A valid or guessable upload_id for a target upload
## Path
ListParts HTTP -> authorize_list_parts -> list_multipart_parts_for_upload -> pg.list_multipart_parts (DB work) -> AccessDenied
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:4256-4292` - authorize_list_parts performs list_multipart_parts_for_upload before requester_can_manage_multipart_upload.
- `crates/storage/src/node.rs:1025-1040` - list_multipart_parts_for_upload loads upload and calls pg.list_multipart_parts immediately.
- `crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:5335-5395` - list_multipart_parts executes SQL queries and materializes part records.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:60-70` - Default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000, indicating deployment exposure is configurable.
## Narrative
The ListParts authorization path calls storage_node.list_multipart_parts_for_upload before requester_can_manage_multipart_upload, so SQLite metadata reads occur even when the requester is unauthorized. In authz.rs, list_multipart_parts_for_upload is invoked before the authz check; storage/node.rs immediately loads the upload and calls pg.list_multipart_parts; pg_store.rs executes SQL queries and materializes rows. This creates a pre-auth resource consumption/timing side effect on unauthorized requests, but does not expose data or bypass authorization.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication and requester identity
- requester_can_manage_multipart_upload authorization check (post-read)
- max_connections/max_inflight_requests limits
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests or ingress configuration in repo; exposure (public vs internal) is uncertain.
- No runtime profiling to quantify cost of list_multipart_parts under load.
